From f2f15826fb81810571c1d4fba819e6a18721ef5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ESP32S3VM intc <intc@esp32s3vm.invalid>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 11:33:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] hw/xtensa/esp32s3_intc: model source OR/remap/disable
 semantics

Rework the ESP32-S3 interrupt matrix from a direct per-source map to the
TRM chapter 9 (Interrupt Matrix) semantics:

- track the asserted level of every peripheral interrupt source
  independently; each CPU external interrupt output is the logical OR of
  the asserted sources whose MAP register selects it, so several sources
  routed to one CPU interrupt cannot clear one another and the line only
  deasserts once every mapped asserted source is gone;
- re-derive all CPU routes on every input edge and MAP write, covering
  remapping of an asserted source on both register files;
- MAP values naming a CPU internal interrupt (6, 7, 11, 15, 16, 29 per
  TRM Table 9.3-2) route nowhere, matching the documented way to detach
  a source (TRM 9.3.3.3; IDF INT_MUX_DISABLED_INTNO == 6); writes stay
  masked to the 5-bit MAP field;
- model INTR_STATUS_0..3 (RO, offsets 0x18C-0x198 per CPU file) as the
  per-file source status snapshot for the documented sources only (word
  3 carries the last 3 sources, TRM Table 9.3-1); inputs beyond
  ETS_MAX_INTR_SOURCE have reserved MAP slots (RAZ/WI) and drive no
  status bit and no CPU interrupt, so asserted stray inputs can neither
  corrupt the snapshot nor spuriously route to external interrupt 0;
- CLOCK_GATE (0x19C, reset 1) freezes the gated CPU file (writes
  ignored, outputs and status held) and the DATE version register
  (0x7FC, reset 0x02012300);
- reset MAP registers to the documented 0x10 default, reserved slots to
  zero (RAZ/WI), and clear inputs/status on reset;
- expose the real matrix-to-CPU interrupt lines as the named GPIO output
  "cpu-int-out" so their levels remain observable (qtest intercept)
  while staying wired to the CPUs; machine wiring unchanged.

Register layout (512 MAP slots per CPU file, CPU0 +0x000 / CPU1 +0x800
of 0x600C2000) is preserved.
